    {
      "content": "<h3>Comparator versus Comparable</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:100%;\">\n<tr><th style=\"text-align:left;\"></th><th style=\"text-align:left;\">Comparator</th><th style=\"text-align:left;\">Comparable</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Method</td><td><code>compare(a, b)</code></td><td><code>compareTo(other)</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Separate object / lambda</td><td>Inside the class</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Orders per class</td><td><strong>Many</strong></td><td>One</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Works on classes you cannot edit</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Helper methods</td><td>comparing, thenComparing, reversed, nullsFirst...</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Package</td><td>java.util</td><td>java.lang</td></tr>\n</table>"
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Real-time practice 1: Sort menu for a fort table (name, height, district then trek time)</h3>\n<p>A web page shows a table of forts with a <strong>\"Sort by\"</strong> menu. Each menu choice is just a different <code>Comparator</code> held in a <code>Map</code>, so adding a new sort needs one line.</p>\n<p><strong>What the program does:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Keep the sort options in a <strong>LinkedHashMap&lt;String, Comparator&gt;</strong>.</li>\n<li>Build them with <strong>comparing / thenComparing / reversed</strong>.</li>\n<li>Apply the chosen comparator to the same list with <strong>list.sort</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>nullsLast</strong> keeps forts without a district at the bottom.</li>\n</ol>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// Real-time practice: a \"Sort by\" menu made of Comparators\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Comparator;\nimport java.util.LinkedHashMap;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\npublic class FortSortMenu {\n    record Fort(String name, String district, int heightMetres, int trekHours) { }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<Fort> forts = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\n            new Fort(\"Torna\", \"Pune\", 1403, 5), new Fort(\"Raigad\", \"Raigad\", 820, 6),\n            new Fort(\"Lohagad\", \"Pune\", 1033, 2), new Fort(\"Panhala\", \"Kolhapur\", 845, 1),\n            new Fort(\"Rajgad\", null, 1376, 4)));\n\n        Map<String, Comparator<Fort>> menu = new LinkedHashMap<>();\n        menu.put(\"Name (A to Z)\", Comparator.comparing(Fort::name));\n        menu.put(\"Height (highest first)\", Comparator.comparingInt(Fort::heightMetres).reversed());\n        menu.put(\"District, then quickest trek\", Comparator\n            .comparing(Fort::district, Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.naturalOrder()))\n            .thenComparingInt(Fort::trekHours));\n\n        for (Map.Entry<String, Comparator<Fort>> option : menu.entrySet()) {\n            forts.sort(option.getValue());\n            System.out.println(\"Sort by \" + option.getKey() + \":\");\n            for (Fort f : forts) {\n                System.out.println(\"   \" + f.name() + \" | \" + f.district() + \" | \" + f.heightMetres() + \" m | \" + f.trekHours() + \" h\");\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// Sort by Name (A to Z):\n//    Lohagad | Pune | 1033 m | 2 h\n//    Panhala | Kolhapur | 845 m | 1 h\n//    Raigad | Raigad | 820 m | 6 h\n//    Rajgad | null | 1376 m | 4 h\n//    Torna | Pune | 1403 m | 5 h\n// Sort by Height (highest first):\n//    Torna | Pune | 1403 m | 5 h\n//    Rajgad | null | 1376 m | 4 h\n//    Lohagad | Pune | 1033 m | 2 h\n//    Panhala | Kolhapur | 845 m | 1 h\n//    Raigad | Raigad | 820 m | 6 h\n// Sort by District, then quickest trek:\n//    Panhala | Kolhapur | 845 m | 1 h\n//    Lohagad | Pune | 1033 m | 2 h\n//    Torna | Pune | 1403 m | 5 h\n//    Raigad | Raigad | 820 m | 6 h\n//    Rajgad | null | 1376 m | 4 h"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Best practices</h3>\n<p>Build comparators from small pieces.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prefer <code>Comparator.comparing(...).thenComparing(...)</code> over hand-written <code>compare</code> methods.</li>\n<li>Use <code>comparingInt / Long / Double</code> for primitive fields; never subtract.</li>\n<li>Add a tie-breaker so the order is total, especially for TreeSet and TreeMap.</li>\n<li>Use <code>nullsFirst / nullsLast</code> when keys can be null.</li>\n<li>Keep reusable comparators as <code>static final</code> constants and give them clear names.</li>\n<li>Remember that <code>reversed()</code> reverses the entire chain built so far.</li>\n</ul>"
    }
